
**Card**
The card class takes an integer value for its constructor and will throw an illegal argument exception if the card is negative. Card also contains a getValue method that returns the value of the card. 

**CardDeck**
CardDeck takes two values for the constructor, one for the number that represents the deck and one for the number of players in the game. The constructor then creates a Card list with a size of 4\*n +1, with n representing the number of players and then sets two variables top and tail to zero. The size of the list is made so that the deck cannot be full; as the players will always hold 4 cards, a single deck can only hold 4n cards at most. An alternative to this would be to prevent players from discarding to a full deck, pausing the Player threads so that they cannot discard, but this approach could cause issues with locking threads, potentially leading to a scenario where the game is completely paused due to deadlock. Having the size larger than necessary prevents any need for pausing threads. 
CardDeck is implemented as a circular queue, with the variable top pointing to the start of the queue and tail pointing to the next empty space. 
The method addCard will take in a new Card and place it in the space tail points at, before incrementing tail, wrapping back around to 0 if the value exceeds the length of the array. 
CardDeck also has the method drawCard for taking a card out, which returns the Card that top is pointing at, before incrementing top. If the queue is empty, then the CardDeck will call for the thread to wait, preventing the Player that draws from the CardDeck from trying to take a null value for Card. The addCard method will notify the waiting thread so that when a Card is added, the waiting Player can draw a Card and resume the thread. 
// notifyHasWon notifies a waiting thread that a player has won - stops action
CardDeck finally has a method printDeckToFile which will output the contents of the Card array to a new text file.

**Player**
Player is a class that inherits from the Thread class built into Java. The constructor for Player takes a number for the value of the Player, two CardDecks, one for drawing from and one for discarding to, an AtomicInteger that represents the number of the Player who wins the game, and a CyclicBarrier that prevents the thread from running until the game begins. The barrier stops the thread from starting until all threads have been created, preventing a Player from taking turns before all other threads have checked their initial hand. The Player constructor then creates a Card array of size 4, since the Player will only ever hold 4 cards due to the draw and discard needing to be one action. An integer value 'wanted' is also created to keep track of the number of cards Player has that it keeps hold of. (Cards that have a value equal to the number Player has been assigned)
The addCard method for Player takes in a Card value and adds it to any empty space in the Player hand. If the Card input is the same value as the Player number then the variable wanted is incremented.
The private discard method will generate a random integer with a maximum value of the number of unwanted cards, (Cards that don't have the same value as the Player number) then go through the array and subtract from the value until 0 and return the Card at that position. This prevents the Player from discarding a wanted Card, while still randomly choosing a Card to discard. This could've been implemented by generating a random number between 0 and 3 and taking the Card at this index, then discarding it if the Card is unwanted or choosing a new random number if the Card is wanted. However, this approach could lead to the same number being generated several times before a Card is chosen, for example when there is only one unwanted Card, it is unlikely the random number will land on that Card. 

Both addCard and discard are methods that are called by atomicDrawDiscard. The purpose of this method is to make drawing and discard one atomic action, so that the number of Cards that Player holds is always exactly 4. When drawing a card, the deck the card is taken from is locked, so that other threads cannot discard into it. Similarly, when discarding, the discard deck is locked so that it is impossible for another thread to draw the Card a Player is currently discarding.
If the draw deck for a Player is empty, the Player releases the lock it has on the CardDeck and waits until notified by the CardDeck that another Player has discarded a Card into the deck, allowing the current Player to draw a Card.
If the action is interrupted by a Player winning, the action is either stopped before any changes are made, or the action is finished and then the thread is stopped. Alternatively, when interrupted the action could be reverted and the drawn card is put back into the CardDeck that it was drawn from. Neither solution has major downsides, but we went with the first as it introduced less complexity.
This method is thread safe as two Players will never be able to see the same Card at the same time or overwrite a Card. Other threads may be able to take actions such as drawing from a CardDeck that a second Player is about to discard to, and has already drawn from it's respective draw deck, but the first Player will never see the Card that the second Player is discarding, as the CardDeck would be locked while that action takes place. Hence there will never be two pointers to the same Card object.

After the method has finished drawing and discarding, the Player will write the action to a text file, appending to the current one.

